.\" mutos_c0.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler front end
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_c0.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_c0.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_C0 1 "October 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_c0 \- MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler, first pass (front end)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_c0
.I source
.I temp1
.I temp2
.RB [ \-P ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_c0
is the first of the two passes that make up the MUTOS 1700 cross C
compiler (the second is
.BR mutos_c1 (1)).
It reads
.IR source ,
normally the output of
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
parses and type-checks it, and writes the tagged intermediate-code
byte stream of the original compiler to
.I temp1
and
.I temp2
(see
.BR OUTPUT ).
.PP
It is a port of the original MUTOS 1700
.IR /lib/c0 ,
read from its binary: Version 7 Unix's
.B c0
as MUTOS 1700 has it, ported to the 8086 \(en v7's functions, in v7's
source order, with the changes the binary shows (its
.B char
conversions, its five-bit conversion codes, its function header and
register handling, a 500-entry symbol table). It runs on an image of
the original's 64K data segment \(en the binary's own initialized data,
every variable at its original address, the heap in the original's
block sizes \(en so that it does what the original does with its memory,
including the original's bugs. It is checked against the original run
under an 8086 emulator, on every golden and on random programs; see
.I docs/C0_PORT_PLAN.md
and
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
in the toolchain source tree.
.PP
It accepts the C the original accepts: the K&R language of Version 7
Unix \(en structures, unions, bit-fields, enumerations,
.BR typedef ,
initializers of any kind, the old
.B =+
assignment operators (with a warning where they are ambiguous) and the
.B \e/
and
.B /\e
operators \(en with
.B short
the same as
.BR int .
.PP
The input may contain comments, lines of the form
\fB#\fR \fIline\fR \fB\(dq\fR\fIfile\fR\fB\(dq\fR
(which set the line number and the file name of the messages) and SOH
bytes (\e001), after which the next newline is not counted as a
line.
.SH OUTPUT
.I temp1
receives the intermediate code: a sequence of tagged items, each either
an operator code (one byte, followed by the fixed byte 0376), a
little-endian 16-bit word, a NUL-terminated symbol name or a floating
constant's text.
.I temp2
receives the string literals: one labelled block of byte values per
literal (a
.B char
array's string initializer is written into
.I temp1
instead, unlabelled, where the declaration is). Both end with an EOFC
item. This is the original
.IR /lib/c0 's
format, byte for byte; it is what
.BR mutos_c1 (1)
reads. The source tree's
.I src/mutos_cc/dump_temp.py
prints either file in readable form.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I source
The input file.
.TP
.I temp1
The output file for the intermediate code.
.TP
.I temp2
The output file for the string literals.
.TP
.B \-P
Any fourth argument (v7's
.BR cc (1)
passes
.B \-P
for
.BR "cc \-p" )
switches on profiling: each function's code starts with a PROFIL item
for its call counter.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages are the original's, on standard error, as
.RI \(dq line ": " message \(dq,
or
.RI \(dq file : line ": " message \(dq
once a
.B #
line has named a file; parsing continues after an error. Warnings
(\(lqWarning: =- operator assumed\(rq, \(lq(Warning only)\(rq, ...)
do not count as errors. The exit status is 0, or 1 if an error was
reported (or the arguments or files were wrong: \(lqArg count\(rq,
\(lqCan't find\(rq, \(lqCan't create temp\(rq).
.PP
Where the original traps, reads past the end of its input for ever, or
reads memory whose contents depend on state the port does not model
(the stdio buffers and FILE structures of the original's
.IR libc.a ),
.B mutos_c0
writes everything before that point, prints
.RI \(dq line ": c0: " reason " ... refused\(dq"
and exits with status 2. These are bugs of the original: a name, a
comment or a
.B #
line that runs into the end of the file; a constant division whose
quotient does not fit (the 8086 divide fault); an initialized array of
zero-length elements; a structure member redeclared with another type;
more alignment holes in a structure than the original's member list
holds; a
.B goto
expression whose tree the original stores over live data or builds
from a dead stack frame's word;
an input byte 0210..0247 outside a string; a number of more than 94
characters. See
.I docs/MUTOS_ORIGINAL_BUGS.md
in the source tree for each, with what the original does.
.SH BUGS
The original's bugs are reproduced, not fixed (see
.BR DIAGNOSTICS ).
.PP
.B mutos_cc,
the driver that would normally chain
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
and
.BR mutos_ld (1)
together into a single pipeline, does not exist yet; invoke this pass
directly (see
.BR SYNOPSIS ),
or see
.I tests/mutos_cc/run_goldens.sh
for a scripted example.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_as (1)
